                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "user": "UBKNXPBAB",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1767910317.416179",
        "client_msg_id": "78cc92ed-8306-48ee-b3da-9d3eede0d9ad",
        "text": "I agree! The original framing of the paper was, in fact, \u201cwhat is programming\u201d, but as I worked on it I realized that this was (duh) way too broad a scope to take on in a week or two of writing. And I realized that, right now, I\u2019m most interested in this narrower scope of blurring programming &amp; direct use.\n\nFWIW, I think <@UDCLA1HU4>\u2019s <http://www.cambridge.org/core/books/cultures-of-programming/075A2D1DE611EE47807A683147B21691|book> takes on the \u201cwhat is programming\u201d question more broadly, adopting a \u201ccultural\u201d lens \u2013 how do different cultures think of programming differently? My paper implicitly adopts the perspective of the humanistic culture (\u201cSocrates\u201d). Very much Chapter-3 stuff.",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1767851065.489519",
        "parent_user_id": "UBKNXPBAB",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "qNba9",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "I agree! The original framing of the paper was, in fact, \u201cwhat is programming\u201d, but as I worked on it I realized that this was (duh) way too broad a scope to take on in a week or two of writing. And I realized that, right now, I\u2019m most interested in this narrower scope of blurring programming & direct use.\n\nFWIW, I think "
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "user",
                                "user_id": "UDCLA1HU4"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "\u2019s "
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "link",
                                "url": "http://www.cambridge.org/core/books/cultures-of-programming/075A2D1DE611EE47807A683147B21691",
                                "text": "book"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": " takes on the \u201cwhat is programming\u201d question more broadly, adopting a \u201ccultural\u201d lens \u2013 how do different cultures think of programming differently? My paper implicitly adopts the perspective of the humanistic culture (\u201cSocrates\u201d). Very much Chapter-3 stuff."
                            }
